WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.830
YURI GRINSHTEYN: When you run
applications in production,

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:04.258
you need to monitor the
infrastructure they run on.

00:00:04.258 --> 00:00:06.675
You also need to measure the
golden signals of application

00:00:06.675 --> 00:00:08.940
health like error
rates and latency.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.670
Today I'm going to show you
how to instrument your service

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.700
to expose application-specific
telemetry with Prometheus

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.340
and how to configure Google's
Manage Prometheus service

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.990
to ingest those metrics.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:20.850
I'm Yuri, an SRE at Google.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.060
Welcome back to "Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud."

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:30.800
Last time, we started using
Google's managed service

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.290
for Prometheus by replacing
the Prometheus image

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:34.970
with Google's forked image.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.340
That enabled ingesting
Prometheus metrics

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:41.360
into the managed back end and
querying them using PromQL.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.360
Those metrics were then also
available in Cloud Monitoring.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.607
And you can start using them in
charts, dashboards, and alerts.

00:00:48.607 --> 00:00:50.690
That was enough to start
collecting infrastructure

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:51.950
metrics from our cluster.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:54.583
Today, we're going to
go two steps further.

00:00:54.583 --> 00:00:56.750
I'm going to show you how
to instrument your service

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:59.510
to expose telemetry and
how to configure Prometheus

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.310
to ingest those metrics.

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:03.980
We'll do that by setting up
Managed Prometheus Collection,

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:07.010
a zero-management way to
run Prometheus and the way

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:08.480
to go if you're
new to Prometheus

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.160
or setting up a new cluster.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.360
All right, let's get into it.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.750
The first step is to
instrument our service itself

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.300
to expose a dedicated
endpoint that Prometheus

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.130
can use to scrape for metrics.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:21.800
We covered this in our previous
episode on custom metrics.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.380
But let's have a quick look
at an example in Python.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.250
We'll of course link to the full
sample in the episode notes.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.440
This is an example of
Python Flask app that

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:32.958
just has a basic home page.

00:01:32.958 --> 00:01:34.500
Let's walk through
the code together.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.140
First, import the
necessary modules.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.350
Then define the metrics
you want to capture.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.100
In this example,
we're going to track

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:45.377
total requests, failed requests,
and a latency distribution.

00:01:45.377 --> 00:01:47.460
We're going to use a
Decorator to time how long it

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.780
takes to load a specific route.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:52.570
And we can increment
the counters as needed.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.650
Finally, we specify the endpoint
where Prometheus metrics

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.090
should be exposed.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:00.420
Now we can see the data
on this /metrics endpoint.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.670
We're now ready to move
on to the second step

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:07.270
and configure Prometheus to
collect and ingest this data.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.770
Let's start by verifying that
our application is ready to be

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.800
monitored by Prometheus.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.200
We can use port forwarding
to confirm that it's exposing

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.360
Prometheus metrics on
the /metrics endpoint.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.870
In order to get our application
monitored by Managed Prometheus

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:30.810
collection, we'll
need to do two things.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.780
First, we'll use
this gcloud command

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.720
to install Managed
Prometheus in our cluster.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.720
Note that you can do this using
kubectl if your cluster is not

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.350
running in GKE.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.990
At this point, we can confirm
that infrastructure collection

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:46.838
is working by querying metrics
that the Managed Prometheus

00:02:46.838 --> 00:02:48.880
collector is ingesting
about itself, for example.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:58.270
Now, we're ready to get
our application monitored

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:00.625
by creating a new pod
monitoring resource.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.610
The selector specifies
which pods to monitor

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:06.540
based on label value.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:12.470
And the endpoints specify
where to scrape for metrics.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:19.610
As we saw before, our
application is exposing

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:23.360
Prometheus metrics on the
/metrics endpoint on port 8080,

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.452
which is called metrics in
a deployment configuration.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:32.680
Once that's applied,
let's run a query

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.972
to confirm that application
metrics are being ingested.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.570
And there we are.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:42.210
We can also query this
metrics in Metrics Explorer

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:43.770
simply by searching
for it like this.

00:03:49.647 --> 00:03:51.730
And that's how you can
instrument your application

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.550
for Prometheus Monitoring and
have its metrics be collected

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:59.420
and ingested by the Google
managed service for Prometheus.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.030
Thanks so much for
watching today.

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:03.530
We learned how to instrument
your application for Prometheus

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.140
monitoring and how to
configure Google's managed

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.900
service for Prometheus
to collect those metrics.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.520
Next time, we'll
have a look at how

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.110
to migrate an
existing Prometheus

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:14.690
deployment to the managed
service for Prometheus.

